<article class="env-lemma" id="01SL">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01SL">Lemma <span data-tag="01SL">29.13.2</span>.</a> A quasi-affine morphism is separated and quasi-compact. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $f : X \to S$ be quasi-affine. Quasi-compactness is immediate from Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01K4" data-tag="01K4">26.19.2</a>. Let $U \subset S$ be an affine open. If we can show that $f^{-1}(U)$ is a separated scheme, then $f$ is separated (Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01KP" data-tag="01KP">26.21.7</a> shows that being separated is local on the base). By assumption $f^{-1}(U)$ is isomorphic to an open subscheme of an affine scheme. An affine scheme is separated and hence every open subscheme of an affine scheme is separated as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>